  "userdoc": {
    "kind": "user",
    "methods": {},
    "notice": "One append-only version log per document (keyed by genesis), with an         agreement/offer layer over the SAME log — publishing and licensing are         one concern. Supersedes S2SPublicationModuleV2: the deal vocabulary         becomes agreement, reviewers are added, and read access moves behind         two explicit gates. Model:   - `makeOffer` is the single write authors use. It appends a version to the     log, then: whitelisted author (or safe) → the document becomes an     AGREEMENT immediately; anyone else → it becomes a pending OFFER. An author     can keep calling `makeOffer` to edit while it is still an offer (each call     appends a version) before the publication agrees.   - `acceptOffer` (onlySafe) promotes an offer to an agreement.   - `retract` is asymmetric: the author withdraws entirely (removed from     agreements/offers); the publication (safe) demotes an agreement back to an     offer, or rejects an offer.   - `_append` does not gate the first version of a genesis. An untrusted     author can always leave a pending offer instead of reverting. Read gates (frozen signatures; sibling branches code against them):   - `canRead(genesis, reader)` — safe or the genesis author, only once the     genesis is under an agreement; a reviewer, for an agreement or an offer.   - `canReview(genesis, who)` — a reviewer, for an agreement or an offer. Reviewers are an enumerable set capped at `MAX_REVIEWERS`, managed by the safe. `agreementsHeldBy` counts live agreements per genesis author and is folded into `canPublish`; `ownerHasAgreement` exposes it. Agreements and offers are enumerable sets of genesis pointing into the one log; `getAgreements`/`getOffers` read each entry's latest version via a cached pointer. Identity is bytes32 everywhere; the string→bytes32 conversion is owned by `shared` and used identically at write time and at the gate, so no on-chain cid map exists.",
    "version": 1
  }
